@anon.814f0bdf02fb37ec5ace91213a028c11.795 = private unnamed_addr constant [805 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for unnecessary generators that can be rewritten as set\0Acomprehensions (or with `set()` directly).\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIt is unnecessary to use `set` around a generator expression, since\0Athere are equivalent comprehensions for these types. Using a\0Acomprehension is clearer and more idiomatic.\0A\0AFurther, if the comprehension can be removed entirely, as in the case of\0A`set(x for x in foo)`, it's better to use `set(foo)` directly, since it's\0Aeven more direct.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aset(f(x) for x in foo)\0Aset(x for x in foo)\0Aset((x for x in foo))\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0A{f(x) for x in foo}\0Aset(foo)\0Aset(foo)\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe, as it may occasionally drop comments\0Awhen rewriting the call. In most cases, though, comments will be preserved.\0A", align 1
