@anon.814f0bdf02fb37ec5ace91213a028c11.900 = private unnamed_addr constant [945 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `for` loops that can be replaced by a dictionary comprehension.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AWhen creating or extending a dictionary in a for-loop, prefer a dictionary\0Acomprehension. Comprehensions are more readable and more performant.\0A\0AFor example, when comparing `{x: x for x in list(range(1000))}` to the `for`\0Aloop version, the comprehension is ~10% faster on Python 3.11.\0A\0ANote that, as with all `perflint` rules, this is only intended as a\0Amicro-optimization, and will have a negligible impact on performance in\0Amost cases.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Apairs = ((\22a\22, 1), (\22b\22, 2))\0Aresult = {}\0Afor x, y in pairs:\0A    if y % 2:\0A        result[x] = y\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Apairs = ((\22a\22, 1), (\22b\22, 2))\0Aresult = {x: y for x, y in pairs if y % 2}\0A```\0A\0AIf you're appending to an existing dictionary, use the `update` method instead:\0A```python\0Apairs = ((\22a\22, 1), (\22b\22, 2))\0Aresult.update({x: y for x, y in pairs if y % 2})\0A```\0A", align 1
