@anon.814f0bdf02fb37ec5ace91213a028c11.967 = private unnamed_addr constant [994 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `if` statements that can be replaced with `min()` or `max()`\0Acalls.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AAn `if` statement that selects the lesser or greater of two sub-expressions\0Acan be replaced with a `min()` or `max()` call respectively. Where possible,\0Aprefer `min()` and `max()`, as they're more concise and readable than the\0Aequivalent `if` statements.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aif score > highest_score:\0A    highest_score = score\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Ahighest_score = max(highest_score, score)\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis fix is marked unsafe if it would delete any comments within the replacement range.\0A\0AAn example to illustrate where comments are preserved and where they are not:\0A\0A```py\0Aa, b = 0, 10\0A\0Aif a >= b: # deleted comment\0A    # deleted comment\0A    a = b # preserved comment\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `max`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#max)\0A- [Python documentation: `min`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#min)\0A", align 1
