@anon.814f0bdf02fb37ec5ace91213a028c11.1027 = private unnamed_addr constant [963 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `else` clauses on loops without a `break` statement.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AWhen a loop includes an `else` statement, the code inside the `else` clause\0Awill be executed if the loop terminates \22normally\22 (i.e., without a\0A`break`).\0A\0AIf a loop _always_ terminates \22normally\22 (i.e., does _not_ contain a\0A`break`), then the `else` clause is redundant, as the code inside the\0A`else` clause will always be executed.\0A\0AIn such cases, the code inside the `else` clause can be moved outside the\0Aloop entirely, and the `else` clause can be removed.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afor item in items:\0A    print(item)\0Aelse:\0A    print(\22All items printed\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afor item in items:\0A    print(item)\0Aprint(\22All items printed\22)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `break` and `continue` Statements, and `else` Clauses on Loops](https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/controlflow.html#break-and-continue-statements-and-else-clauses-on-loops)\0A", align 1
