@anon.814f0bdf02fb37ec5ace91213a028c11.1031 = private unnamed_addr constant [1823 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `TypedDict` declarations that use functional syntax.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A`TypedDict` types can be defined either through a functional syntax\0A(`Foo = TypedDict(...)`) or a class syntax (`class Foo(TypedDict): ...`).\0A\0AThe class syntax is more readable and generally preferred over the\0Afunctional syntax.\0A\0ANonetheless, there are some situations in which it is impossible to use\0Athe class-based syntax. This rule will not apply to those cases. Namely,\0Ait is impossible to use the class-based syntax if any `TypedDict` fields are:\0A- Not valid [python identifiers] (for example, `@x`)\0A- [Python keywords] such as `in`\0A- [Private names] such as `__id` that would undergo [name mangling] at runtime\0A  if the class-based syntax was used\0A- [Dunder names] such as `__int__` that can confuse type checkers if they're used\0A  with the class-based syntax.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom typing import TypedDict\0A\0AFoo = TypedDict(\22Foo\22, {\22a\22: int, \22b\22: str})\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom typing import TypedDict\0A\0A\0Aclass Foo(TypedDict):\0A    a: int\0A    b: str\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe if there are any comments within the\0Arange of the `TypedDict` definition, as these will be dropped by the\0Aautofix.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `typing.TypedDict`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/typing.html#typing.TypedDict)\0A\0A[Private names]: https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/classes.html#private-variables\0A[name mangling]: https://docs.python.org/3/reference/expressions.html#private-name-mangling\0A[python identifiers]: https://docs.python.org/3/reference/lexical_analysis.html#identifiers\0A[Python keywords]: https://docs.python.org/3/reference/lexical_analysis.html#keywords\0A[Dunder names]: https://docs.python.org/3/reference/lexical_analysis.html#reserved-classes-of-identifiers\0A", align 1
